The inverse of a matrix is a matrix which, when multiplied by the original matrix, results in the identity matrix. For two matrices to be inverses of each other, their product must equal the identity matrix. Therefore, we have:
\[
\begin{pmatrix} a & 1 & b \\ 2 & 2 & 3 \\ c & 5 & d \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} -5 & e & -11 \\ f & -13 & g \\ 2 & h & 4 \end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
\]
By calculating the product, we can find the values of the variables. For instance, the top-left entry of the product matrix is $-5a + f + 2b$, which must equal 1, and the top-middle entry is $ae - 13 + 2h$, which must equal 0. Similarly, we can calculate the other entries and set them equal to the corresponding entries in the identity matrix. Solving this system of equations, we find that:
\[a = -1, b = 1, e = -13, f = 2, c = 1, g = 4, h = -1, d = -1\]
To solve this system of equations, we equate entries in the resulting matrix to the corresponding entries in the identity matrix. For example, the top-left entry of the product matrix is $a(-5) + 2f + b(2) = 1$. This simplifies to $-5a + 2f + 2b = 1$. Similarly, we can equate all other entries to get a system of eight equations in eight variables. Solving this system, we find that $a = -1, b = 1, e = -13, f = 2, c = 1, g = 4, h = -1, d = -1$. The sum of the variables is then $-1 + 1 + (-13) + 2 + 1 + 4 + (-1) + (-1) = -12 + 2 = -10 + 2 = -8$.</think> <answer>8 </answer>

